00:33:08.050 --> 00:33:10.450
It's not, I mean, we would sometimes go in

00:33:10.450 --> 00:33:13.710
and just like three or
four hour long games.

00:33:13.710 --> 00:33:16.110
'Cause it kept going back and forth

00:33:16.110 --> 00:33:18.700
and everyone who was playing,

00:33:18.700 --> 00:33:20.260
like you think I'm competitive.

00:33:20.260 --> 00:33:25.090
I was easily the least
competitive in this group,

00:33:25.090 --> 00:33:26.970
between my father-in-law
and my brother-in-law

00:33:26.970 --> 00:33:28.710
and my wife's cousin.

00:33:28.710 --> 00:33:31.230
As long as everybody's
into it, that's great.

00:33:31.230 --> 00:33:32.910
No, but what would happen is,

00:33:32.910 --> 00:33:34.900
and this is why I wanna get
back to asking you about,

00:33:34.900 --> 00:33:38.313
'cause this maps onto like,
the way my family plays games,

00:33:40.440 --> 00:33:43.510
it would get my father-in-law
and my brother-in-law,

00:33:43.510 --> 00:33:45.267
would sometimes, I was like,

00:33:45.267 --> 00:33:48.328
"They're gonna fight,
they're finally gonna fight."

00:33:48.328 --> 00:33:49.161
You know what I'm saying?

00:33:49.161 --> 00:33:49.994
Like fist fight.

00:33:49.994 --> 00:33:51.913
'Cause it was so intense.

00:33:52.783 --> 00:33:53.616
It would get so intense.

00:33:53.616 --> 00:33:56.350
And like you do these
things that like with Risk,

00:33:56.350 --> 00:33:58.983
you have the option of like,

00:34:00.200 --> 00:34:03.810
I'm gonna team up with this guy
and we're gonna sabotage you

00:34:03.810 --> 00:34:04.880
and we're gonna take you out.

00:34:04.880 --> 00:34:06.550
And a lot of times we would
do that to my father-in-law,

00:34:06.550 --> 00:34:08.092
because he was the best at the game.

00:34:08.092 --> 00:34:08.925
He's been playing for a long time,

00:34:08.925 --> 00:34:10.420
you know how he's so competitive.

00:34:10.420 --> 00:34:11.470
And so--

00:34:11.470 --> 00:34:12.410
That made,

00:34:12.410 --> 00:34:13.690
nothing makes you angry than

00:34:13.690 --> 00:34:16.027
if you get teamed up against him,

00:34:16.027 --> 00:34:16.867
there's nothing, there's
little you can do.

00:34:16.867 --> 00:34:18.760
And I was always just kinda like,

00:34:18.760 --> 00:34:20.310
a little bit like Switzerland.

00:34:20.310 --> 00:34:23.460
I didn't start in Switzerland,
that's not a great strategy.

00:34:23.460 --> 00:34:27.430
But I'm really competitive,

00:34:27.430 --> 00:34:28.920
but I would just kinda play it.

00:34:28.920 --> 00:34:29.753
I was just like,

00:34:29.753 --> 00:34:31.607
"This is entertaining because

00:34:31.607 --> 00:34:34.830
"they're so passionate about this."

00:34:34.830 --> 00:34:36.470
And if you go to somebody who's like,

00:34:36.470 --> 00:34:37.710
they've got their strategy down.

00:34:37.710 --> 00:34:40.470
You can't just, like that couple,

00:34:40.470 --> 00:34:44.920
did you a disservice by
inviting you into a game

00:34:44.920 --> 00:34:48.040
that can be as intense as Risk

00:34:48.040 --> 00:34:49.820
and not like picking up on your signals

00:34:49.820 --> 00:34:51.300
that you were not into it

00:34:51.300 --> 00:34:55.797
or that maybe we should like
play it in a more inviting way.

00:34:56.640 --> 00:34:59.320
It reminds me of the story of when we,

00:34:59.320 --> 00:35:00.920
back in 19, oh not,

00:35:00.920 --> 00:35:03.480
it was 2003 probably,

00:35:03.480 --> 00:35:06.240
when we were on our staff training

00:35:06.240 --> 00:35:07.960
to go on staff with Campus Crusade.

00:35:07.960 --> 00:35:09.290
Yeah, we went down to Florida.

00:35:09.290 --> 00:35:14.290
So we were there like taking
seminary classes for a summer.

00:35:14.810 --> 00:35:16.900
And then also being trained

00:35:16.900 --> 00:35:18.047
in like everything we were gonna do.

00:35:18.047 --> 00:35:19.790
And we became really good friends

00:35:19.790 --> 00:35:23.730
with Eric and Natalie
who were from Tennessee

00:35:23.730 --> 00:35:26.850
and the six of us really got along.

00:35:26.850 --> 00:35:28.370
We're all basically newlyweds I would say.

00:35:28.370 --> 00:35:31.810
We were newlyweds and, and we just,

00:35:31.810 --> 00:35:33.866
we all liked each other.

00:35:33.866 --> 00:35:35.640
And so we connected with them

00:35:35.640 --> 00:35:37.470
probably more than
anybody at staff training.

00:35:37.470 --> 00:35:39.110
And we got into playing, not a board game,

00:35:39.110 --> 00:35:40.840
but a game, party game Catch Phrase.

00:35:40.840 --> 00:35:42.560
And the reason why we played that,

00:35:42.560 --> 00:35:44.240
'cause by that point,

00:35:44.240 --> 00:35:47.490
again that was around the same
time as the Risk incident.

00:35:47.490 --> 00:35:51.390
And like in college, we
would play Risks some.

00:35:51.390 --> 00:35:53.060
Like there were people who liked Risk.

00:35:53.060 --> 00:35:54.120
And now I was like by the,

00:35:54.120 --> 00:35:58.140
I was became convinced that
like, none of this was for me,

00:35:58.140 --> 00:36:01.000
none of these board games, strategy games,

00:36:01.000 --> 00:36:03.383
but party games like Catch
Phrase where it's like,

00:36:03.383 --> 00:36:07.250
it's quick and it's verbal and it's fun.

00:36:07.250 --> 00:36:08.290
And then it's over.

00:36:08.290 --> 00:36:11.720
And you can basically end
it any time, it's not this,

00:36:11.720 --> 00:36:13.850
like you don't have to
invest the whole night in it.

00:36:13.850 --> 00:36:16.390
And then if it goes sideways
or somebody gets upset.

00:36:16.390 --> 00:36:18.040
The learning curve is much lower.

00:36:18.040 --> 00:36:18.873
It's simple.

00:36:18.873 --> 00:36:20.330
You sit down and you get it.

00:36:20.330 --> 00:36:21.990
And then, but we--

00:36:21.990 --> 00:36:23.380
I was very into,

00:36:23.380 --> 00:36:25.520
and still am very into
something like Catch Phrase.

00:36:25.520 --> 00:36:28.360
And we say this all the time,

00:36:28.360 --> 00:36:29.390
you don't think you're competitive.

00:36:29.390 --> 00:36:31.740
I think you're just as competitive as me.

00:36:31.740 --> 00:36:33.000
You just show it in a different way.

00:36:33.000 --> 00:36:35.560
But I think you hate to
lose just as much as me,

00:36:35.560 --> 00:36:37.300
you're just better at
keeping it to yourself.

00:36:37.300 --> 00:36:41.860
Now, and you would get super, super into--

00:36:41.860 --> 00:36:43.820
I don't know about that
but we can come back to it.

00:36:43.820 --> 00:36:45.340
So we would start playing this,

00:36:45.340 --> 00:36:48.920
the six of us and we would
get so into it, like loud.

00:36:48.920 --> 00:36:49.810
We were at the, what was it?

00:36:49.810 --> 00:36:52.449
The El Caribe, was that
the hotel that we were in--

00:36:52.449 --> 00:36:54.990
It was like an apartment building.

00:36:54.990 --> 00:36:56.290
I mean, everyone,

00:36:56.290 --> 00:36:57.820
do we need to explain
what Catch Phrase is?

00:36:57.820 --> 00:37:00.480
Basically, it's hot potato, but like you,

00:37:00.480 --> 00:37:01.650
it's like password.

00:37:01.650 --> 00:37:03.180
There's a word you've
gotta get people to guess.

00:37:03.180 --> 00:37:05.490
And you can't say the word.

00:37:05.490 --> 00:37:07.540
And if it stops in your hands you lose.

00:37:08.475 --> 00:37:11.540
So we, there was this other couple

00:37:11.540 --> 00:37:13.150
that we didn't really know that well,

00:37:13.150 --> 00:37:14.083
but they were on,

00:37:15.010 --> 00:37:17.660
they were gonna go on staff
with athletes and action,

00:37:17.660 --> 00:37:22.320
which is the Campus Crusade
wing that ministers to athletes.

00:37:22.320 --> 00:37:23.170
Yeah, they were like--

00:37:23.170 --> 00:37:25.500
They had been college athletes.

00:37:25.500 --> 00:37:27.200
And so we were like, these
people are competitive.

00:37:27.200 --> 00:37:32.090
They surely will be
into how wild and crazy

00:37:32.090 --> 00:37:35.530
and intense our games of Catch Phrase get.

00:37:35.530 --> 00:37:37.343
We invited them over one night.

00:37:38.180 --> 00:37:40.160
And we're playing the game

00:37:40.160 --> 00:37:42.880
and we're doing our typical like yelling.

00:37:42.880 --> 00:37:44.840
And like when somebody gets
it wrong everybody yells,

00:37:44.840 --> 00:37:47.410
when somebody gets it
right everybody yells.

00:37:47.410 --> 00:37:49.520
And I'm just kinda
noticing this couple is--

00:37:49.520 --> 00:37:50.353
Shrinking.

00:37:50.353 --> 00:37:53.090
They're not really being loud at all.

00:37:53.090 --> 00:37:56.090
They're not, they don't seem
like they have the will to win.

00:37:56.940 --> 00:38:01.460
And I think we scarred them forever.

00:38:01.460 --> 00:38:03.090
They did not ask to come back

00:38:04.900 --> 00:38:06.710
and we didn't talk a whole lot after.

00:38:06.710 --> 00:38:11.570
I think that the energy
we had, it was like,

00:38:11.570 --> 00:38:15.343
it was just, it was fun to have energy.

00:38:16.450 --> 00:38:17.730
We were playing guys against girls.

00:38:17.730 --> 00:38:20.960
And I think, one of the
things that happens with me

00:38:20.960 --> 00:38:23.430
when I'm playing a game is I get,

00:38:23.430 --> 00:38:26.250
I'll say things like, is he mad?

00:38:26.250 --> 00:38:27.810
He seems mad?

00:38:27.810 --> 00:38:28.780
And if you're the kind of person

00:38:28.780 --> 00:38:29.780
who that kind of inner--

00:38:29.780 --> 00:38:30.720
Comedic effect--

00:38:30.720 --> 00:38:31.553
That kind of energy.

00:38:31.553 --> 00:38:32.386
If he knew you.

00:38:32.386 --> 00:38:33.219
Well, no, I'm just,

00:38:33.219 --> 00:38:34.630
I'm really getting into it.

00:38:34.630 --> 00:38:37.630
But the moment that like,
we got to order a pizza.

00:38:37.630 --> 00:38:38.680
I'm like, what y'all want on the pizza?

00:38:38.680 --> 00:38:40.960
You know what I'm saying, it's
like, I'm not actually mad.

00:38:40.960 --> 00:38:43.760
I'm just really intensely competing.

00:38:43.760 --> 00:38:45.830
It's like, when you're playing
basketball with somebody

00:38:45.830 --> 00:38:47.310
and you're like talking trash to them,

00:38:47.310 --> 00:38:48.143
but then you're like,

00:38:48.143 --> 00:38:50.470
"Hey, let's leave in the same car."

00:38:50.470 --> 00:38:52.730
It's just part of the
competitive spirit for me.

00:38:52.730 --> 00:38:55.520
But some people don't
respond well to that.

00:38:55.520 --> 00:38:56.687
And they think,

00:38:56.687 --> 00:39:00.340
"Oh, these people must
be mad at each other."

00:39:00.340 --> 00:39:01.950
And I think that, that wasn't how they're,

00:39:01.950 --> 00:39:03.130
they were also newlyweds.

00:39:03.130 --> 00:39:04.870
I don't think that's
how it worked for them.

00:39:04.870 --> 00:39:06.350
Whereas in our marriages,

00:39:06.350 --> 00:39:08.343
I feel like there was a lot of like,

00:39:09.900 --> 00:39:12.010
you could be loud with your spouse

00:39:12.010 --> 00:39:14.330
and it didn't mean you were mad with them.

00:39:14.330 --> 00:39:16.410
I mean, that's definitely how
me and Jessie are, you know.

00:39:16.410 --> 00:39:19.430
I think also when you
and I were on a team,

00:39:19.430 --> 00:39:23.566
we could give clues
that seemed impossible.

00:39:23.566 --> 00:39:24.399
'Cause we knew how we were doing--

00:39:24.399 --> 00:39:26.160
It's like we would speak in code

00:39:26.160 --> 00:39:28.888
and then it would be very frustrating--

00:39:28.888 --> 00:39:29.721
We were tough to beat.

00:39:29.721 --> 00:39:31.830
We we're tough to beat when
we're on the same team.

00:39:31.830 --> 00:39:32.663
Yes, that was very,

00:39:32.663 --> 00:39:35.350
that was hilarious to us

00:39:35.350 --> 00:39:39.030
and very decimating and frustrating too.

00:39:39.030 --> 00:39:40.010
But so when you're playing--

00:39:40.010 --> 00:39:40.843
To competitors--

00:39:40.843 --> 00:39:42.630
Catan as a family--

00:39:42.630 --> 00:39:44.380
They ended up just not coming around.

00:39:44.380 --> 00:39:48.000
Right, so when you're playing
Catan as a family, are you,

00:39:48.000 --> 00:39:50.730
it sounds to me, what I
was picturing in my mind,

00:39:50.730 --> 00:39:54.230
as you were talking about
it, isn't like, this is,

00:39:54.230 --> 00:39:55.250
I want to win.

00:39:55.250 --> 00:39:56.480
And that's the main
thing I'm thinking about.

00:39:56.480 --> 00:39:58.930
It was more, I'm having a
great time with my family.

00:39:58.930 --> 00:40:00.920
That's what I was feeling.

00:40:00.920 --> 00:40:02.320
Lincoln is pretty competitive

00:40:02.320 --> 00:40:04.090
and he's a very strategic thinker.

00:40:04.090 --> 00:40:06.980
I don't get that
competitive directly with,

00:40:06.980 --> 00:40:08.300
I mean, I get competitive

00:40:08.300 --> 00:40:13.300
for the sake of like entertaining
myself and my family,

00:40:13.550 --> 00:40:15.180
but like not in the same way that,

00:40:15.180 --> 00:40:17.070
on game night, like I want to win.

00:40:17.070 --> 00:40:18.580
And it becomes personal.

00:40:18.580 --> 00:40:21.310
With my family it's like,
if Shepherd wins this game,

00:40:21.310 --> 00:40:22.640
that's awesome for him.

00:40:22.640 --> 00:40:27.160
So I'm not gonna try to like take him out.

00:40:27.160 --> 00:40:29.510
Well, I actually won.

00:40:29.510 --> 00:40:32.280
But I wasn't like gloating or anything.

00:40:32.280 --> 00:40:33.510
I was actually surprised.

00:40:33.510 --> 00:40:36.233
'Cause I, Lincoln started,

00:40:37.310 --> 00:40:39.800
he built all of his
cities in this one place

00:40:39.800 --> 00:40:42.110
where like with,

00:40:42.110 --> 00:40:44.760
odds are he would end up
getting all these bricks.

00:40:44.760 --> 00:40:46.840
He became, he was talking
all this smack about,

00:40:46.840 --> 00:40:48.607
hey, he was a brick factory.

00:40:48.607 --> 00:40:50.085
"Oh, I got so much brick.

00:40:50.085 --> 00:40:53.043
"If anybody wants any brick,
you gotta come to me."

00:40:53.043 --> 00:40:54.970
That became fun, when it was just like,

00:40:54.970 --> 00:40:57.450
you start to play a role
in the game of like,

00:40:57.450 --> 00:41:02.450
you're the brick mogul
and then people think

00:41:02.840 --> 00:41:05.380
he's doing so well 'cause
he's got so much brick

00:41:05.380 --> 00:41:07.050
that nobody wants to trade with him.

00:41:07.050 --> 00:41:11.290
And then he gets frustrated
and that was fun.

00:41:11.290 --> 00:41:12.460
Fun to watch people get frustrated.

00:41:12.460 --> 00:41:13.523
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:41:17.249 --> 00:41:20.590
We were all doing the same
thing and having a good time,

00:41:20.590 --> 00:41:24.270
so then I'm coming, I'm
like, this is a new,

00:41:24.270 --> 00:41:25.573
this is a new era.

00:41:27.570 --> 00:41:28.403
I think we can do this.

00:41:28.403 --> 00:41:31.340
At the beginning of
quarantine, I was thinking,

00:41:31.340 --> 00:41:34.730
you know what, it's puzzle
time, it's game time.

00:41:34.730 --> 00:41:37.580
And on the Mythical Society,

00:41:37.580 --> 00:41:39.110
we were doing this thing early on

00:41:39.110 --> 00:41:41.600
where all the Mythical crew members

00:41:41.600 --> 00:41:43.443
were making videos from home about

00:41:43.443 --> 00:41:45.510
like what they were
doing during quarantine

00:41:45.510 --> 00:41:48.030
and making suggestions about things

00:41:48.030 --> 00:41:53.030
that you could also enjoy while
being isolated in quarantine

00:41:55.630 --> 00:41:58.510
and Caleb, our Graphic Designer,

00:41:58.510 --> 00:42:01.040
who give him credit for most,

00:42:01.040 --> 00:42:04.060
all of the merch stuff that's designed.

00:42:04.060 --> 00:42:06.540
He does the vast majority of that.

00:42:06.540 --> 00:42:09.090
I happen to be watching one
of his videos on the Society.

00:42:09.090 --> 00:42:10.660
And he started talking about board games

00:42:10.660 --> 00:42:13.430
and he talked about this
one game called PARKS.

00:42:13.430 --> 00:42:17.520
And the way he talked about
it was a strategy game

00:42:17.520 --> 00:42:19.630
where you go through,

00:42:19.630 --> 00:42:21.930
when you make your way through
all the national parks.

00:42:21.930 --> 00:42:24.977
And I'm like, "My dream is to like,

00:42:24.977 --> 00:42:27.587
"be the dad who goes to
all the national parks

00:42:27.587 --> 00:42:28.630
"with my family."

00:42:28.630 --> 00:42:30.230
While never leaving my living room.

00:42:30.230 --> 00:42:32.030
So now we can do that here.

00:42:32.030 --> 00:42:34.520
And it's like, the
artwork look really cool.

00:42:34.520 --> 00:42:37.210
And then it was, ended
up I couldn't get one.

00:42:37.210 --> 00:42:39.780
I had to, they have a Kickstarter
campaign for an expansion.

00:42:39.780 --> 00:42:41.220
And I ordered that.

00:42:41.220 --> 00:42:43.897
I still haven't gotten it, but I was like,

00:42:43.897 --> 00:42:45.737
"You know what, we're
gonna get this PARKS game.

00:42:45.737 --> 00:42:49.110
"And this is gonna be
the new family thing."

00:42:49.110 --> 00:42:51.420
Hasn't happened, but I do,

00:42:51.420 --> 00:42:52.950
that's the next thing I'm excited about.

00:42:52.950 --> 00:42:55.597
We come back from the beach and I'm like,

00:42:55.597 --> 00:43:00.137
"All right, every Sunday
that's gonna be our game time."

00:43:01.150 --> 00:43:05.423
And so, you know me, I'm
like, I'm excited about Catan.

00:43:06.270 --> 00:43:08.350
That's what we're gonna play.

00:43:08.350 --> 00:43:10.790
And I thought everybody
else would be excited.

00:43:10.790 --> 00:43:14.090
This is the new Neil
thing, tabletop gaming.

00:43:14.090 --> 00:43:17.327
And then Lily is like, "I
don't want to play that,

00:43:17.327 --> 00:43:18.450
"that game stupid."

00:43:18.450 --> 00:43:20.523
I was like, it wasn't stupid two days ago.

00:43:20.523 --> 00:43:24.080
She was like "Shape and
bricks, it's not for me."

00:43:24.080 --> 00:43:26.150
You ran into a teenage mentality.

00:43:26.150 --> 00:43:27.940
I'm like, you just made me so mad.

00:43:27.940 --> 00:43:30.133
I was like, well, we're gonna play a game.

00:43:31.070 --> 00:43:33.070
And we ended up playing the star,

00:43:33.070 --> 00:43:36.890
we had a Star Wars version
of clue, which that,

00:43:36.890 --> 00:43:38.660
we didn't even finish.

00:43:38.660 --> 00:43:40.690
I was like, look at what happened.

00:43:40.690 --> 00:43:41.523
Fizzle out.

00:43:41.523 --> 00:43:44.280
Fizzle out, and then Lincoln's a few,

00:43:44.280 --> 00:43:48.990
the next weekend Lincoln's
like, "We should play among us."

00:43:48.990 --> 00:43:50.680
I'm like "Tabletop game."

00:43:50.680 --> 00:43:53.603
He was like, "No, we each
need to be on our phones.

00:43:54.607 --> 00:43:57.747
"It's a video game on
your phone that you play.

00:43:57.747 --> 00:43:59.227
"It's fun with five people,

00:43:59.227 --> 00:44:00.767
"but you can't talk to each other.

00:44:00.767 --> 00:44:01.987
"And even though we're in the same room,

00:44:01.987 --> 00:44:03.931
"you gotta act like we're
like in different rooms."

00:44:06.290 --> 00:44:07.710
Wow, okay, yeah.

00:44:07.710 --> 00:44:10.610
And I'm like, seriously, I was,

00:44:10.610 --> 00:44:12.910
I told Lily, I was like, if we play Clue,

00:44:12.910 --> 00:44:15.233
next time we are gonna go back to Catan,

00:44:16.090 --> 00:44:17.890
and we're gonna like it.

00:44:17.890 --> 00:44:18.723
We are going to like it.

00:44:18.723 --> 00:44:19.810
That's the dad spirit.

00:44:19.810 --> 00:44:23.080
And then we didn't get back,
now we're playing among us,

00:44:23.080 --> 00:44:27.410
which is actually a pretty,
it's a very fun game--

00:44:27.410 --> 00:44:28.290
Is it one of those games where like,

00:44:28.290 --> 00:44:29.670
you're all looking at the television,

00:44:29.670 --> 00:44:31.810
like those trivia games
that are on your phone?

00:44:31.810 --> 00:44:36.070
You're all looking at
this spaceship schematic.

00:44:36.070 --> 00:44:40.960
And you're running around
and you're a crew member,

00:44:40.960 --> 00:44:42.830
but then one person is an imposter

00:44:42.830 --> 00:44:44.730
and he's sabotaging everything on the ship

00:44:44.730 --> 00:44:46.120
and killing people.

00:44:46.120 --> 00:44:51.120
And then you have to, if
you're a crew member and again,

00:44:51.540 --> 00:44:53.540
every time you play the game,
you're randomly assigned.

00:44:53.540 --> 00:44:56.920
You have to perform tasks
to like run around the ship

00:44:56.920 --> 00:44:58.040
and do things.

00:44:58.040 --> 00:44:59.470
But if you're the imposter,

00:44:59.470 --> 00:45:00.970
you have to act like you're doing things.

00:45:00.970 --> 00:45:02.440
But then when you're along with somebody,

00:45:02.440 --> 00:45:03.300
you have to kill him.

00:45:03.300 --> 00:45:04.770
And then when you,

00:45:04.770 --> 00:45:07.300
when a crew member finds someone dead,

00:45:07.300 --> 00:45:09.030
they have to report dead body.

00:45:09.030 --> 00:45:11.920
And then everybody comes
together and you discuss,

00:45:11.920 --> 00:45:13.510
like, you actually can talk at that point

00:45:13.510 --> 00:45:16.250
about who you think the imposter is.

00:45:16.250 --> 00:45:20.993
So it's got like a mafia
werewolf kind of vibe, I think.

00:45:21.910 --> 00:45:25.130
But it's mostly in this gaming world.

00:45:25.130 --> 00:45:26.740
Of course I'm so freaking oblivious

00:45:26.740 --> 00:45:28.410
to how to play a video game.

00:45:28.410 --> 00:45:30.420
That they're just laughing
at me the whole time.

00:45:30.420 --> 00:45:31.990
And then ultimately getting angry at me

00:45:31.990 --> 00:45:34.663
because I don't know what
a dead body looks like.

00:45:36.060 --> 00:45:37.540
I don't know how to move around.

00:45:37.540 --> 00:45:40.050
They're like, he was standing
still for a really long time.

00:45:40.050 --> 00:45:43.970
He must have been, he must be the killer.

00:45:43.970 --> 00:45:45.221
No, I don't know how to move.

00:45:45.221 --> 00:45:46.480
It's like, I don't know how to move.

00:45:46.480 --> 00:45:47.803
Yeah, I got stuck.

00:45:48.730 --> 00:45:50.550
So we quickly moved.

00:45:50.550 --> 00:45:53.960
Somehow my family has taken
us away from our new identity

00:45:53.960 --> 00:45:57.340
as a board gaming family back
into like our own phone zone.

00:45:57.340 --> 00:46:00.240
Well, but I think this is still a victory

00:46:00.240 --> 00:46:02.520
to be celebrated because first of all,

00:46:02.520 --> 00:46:05.250
the distinction between
board games and party games

00:46:05.250 --> 00:46:06.167
is one that--

00:46:06.167 --> 00:46:08.223
And video games, blurring,

00:46:09.230 --> 00:46:11.260
No, I'm just saying that when it comes to

00:46:11.260 --> 00:46:13.220
what it is that you're
trying to accomplish,

00:46:13.220 --> 00:46:15.800
which is doing something,

00:46:15.800 --> 00:46:17.150
I'm not gonna say not on screens

00:46:17.150 --> 00:46:19.750
because that is on screen,
but doing something together,

00:46:19.750 --> 00:46:22.200
that's fun as a family,
creating family memories,

00:46:23.120 --> 00:46:25.450
the distinction between the
board games and party games.

00:46:25.450 --> 00:46:26.610
It doesn't matter.

00:46:26.610 --> 00:46:28.840
I was happy that most everybody

00:46:28.840 --> 00:46:32.490
was willing to play that
game at least three times

00:46:32.490 --> 00:46:34.253
until it was like, somebody was like,

00:46:34.253 --> 00:46:35.478
I don't wanna do this anymore.

00:46:35.478 --> 00:46:36.390
I going to my room,

00:46:36.390 --> 00:46:37.800
you can't go to your room.

00:46:37.800 --> 00:46:39.670
The thing that you're always dealing with

00:46:39.670 --> 00:46:44.670
when it comes to teenagers
is them being on their phones

00:46:45.420 --> 00:46:47.060
and then being in contact

00:46:47.060 --> 00:46:49.890
with their friends, girlfriends,

00:46:49.890 --> 00:46:52.593
whatever, the people that
they stay in contact with.

00:46:53.560 --> 00:46:55.760
Their attention spans are really short.

00:46:55.760 --> 00:46:59.110
And it's very difficult for them to unplug

00:46:59.110 --> 00:47:00.523
from being in contact.

00:47:01.873 --> 00:47:03.960
And also just their attention
spans are short in general.

00:47:03.960 --> 00:47:06.830
I mean, we watch a lot of movies

00:47:06.830 --> 00:47:09.530
and I try to introduce the kids

00:47:09.530 --> 00:47:12.823
to movies that I grew up with and,

00:47:14.010 --> 00:47:15.640
Locke and I watch horror movies together.

00:47:15.640 --> 00:47:16.730
And now we've kinda gotten,

00:47:16.730 --> 00:47:17.820
you know he's 16 now

00:47:17.820 --> 00:47:22.530
and now we're watching some
of the movies from my list

00:47:22.530 --> 00:47:24.410
of my favorite movies that

00:47:24.410 --> 00:47:26.700
I wouldn't let him watch up until now.

00:47:26.700 --> 00:47:29.770
And when you get, when you're looking at

00:47:29.770 --> 00:47:32.870
a total running time of like
two and a half, three hours,

00:47:32.870 --> 00:47:34.680
for some of these movies,

00:47:34.680 --> 00:47:37.762
that is a lifetime for
a teenager these days-

00:47:37.762 --> 00:47:42.150
Yeah, I told my kids, I'm like,

00:47:42.150 --> 00:47:43.680
you don't know how to watch a movie.

00:47:43.680 --> 00:47:44.710
You can't do it.

00:47:44.710 --> 00:47:46.410
You can watch a show--

00:47:46.410 --> 00:47:49.010
Well, but what they'll do is

00:47:49.010 --> 00:47:51.209
they will binge a television show

00:47:51.209 --> 00:47:54.410
and they'll sit down for five or six hours

00:47:54.410 --> 00:47:57.180
and watch a whole season
of a television show.

00:47:57.180 --> 00:48:00.870
But the feeling of them
having some sort of control

00:48:00.870 --> 00:48:04.330
of them being able to like
take a break between episodes,

00:48:04.330 --> 00:48:06.130
because it's naturally broken up that,

00:48:06.130 --> 00:48:11.130
I mean, that's why in theory I know Quibi

00:48:12.190 --> 00:48:14.240
had had a really bad launch,

00:48:14.240 --> 00:48:18.170
but like something like Quibi
made a lot of sense on paper

00:48:18.170 --> 00:48:21.620
because it was, a it
was designed for mobile.

00:48:21.620 --> 00:48:23.000
I watched a couple of shows on there

00:48:23.000 --> 00:48:26.690
and like the whole like
flipping to vertical

00:48:26.690 --> 00:48:28.910
and the way that that
actually changed the interact,

00:48:28.910 --> 00:48:31.170
it changed it in a way that was cooler

00:48:31.170 --> 00:48:32.623
than I expected it to be.

00:48:34.090 --> 00:48:37.730
But also the fact that,
depending on the show,

00:48:37.730 --> 00:48:40.310
it's like, this is broken
up into byte sized pieces,

00:48:40.310 --> 00:48:41.910
but it's telling a longer story.

00:48:42.780 --> 00:48:44.500
I mean, I didn't get
into any of the content,

00:48:44.500 --> 00:48:46.010
but only tried a couple of things.

00:48:46.010 --> 00:48:47.780
But in theory it made sense.

00:48:47.780 --> 00:48:49.320
It's like, let's go mobile.

00:48:49.320 --> 00:48:52.170
Let's have a bias towards vertical.

00:48:52.170 --> 00:48:55.350
Let's make it into byte sized pieces

00:48:55.350 --> 00:48:58.100
that I don't understand, it just,

00:48:58.100 --> 00:49:02.170
they got a bunch of great
talent to be in the shows

00:49:02.170 --> 00:49:03.950
and to make the shows,

00:49:03.950 --> 00:49:05.830
all that to say is what
I've observed is that

00:49:05.830 --> 00:49:06.860
the thing that you're battling

00:49:06.860 --> 00:49:08.930
is the teenager attention span.

00:49:08.930 --> 00:49:11.924
And so board games fit right into that.

00:49:11.924 --> 00:49:15.350
I can't even get a kid
to watch a movie with me.

00:49:15.350 --> 00:49:17.240
We just what, I was just like,

00:49:17.240 --> 00:49:19.090
I'll say, "Hey, let's watch a movie."

00:49:19.090 --> 00:49:19.970
And they're like, eh.

00:49:19.970 --> 00:49:22.130
I'm like, "Okay, well let's watch a show."

00:49:22.130 --> 00:49:23.100
They're like, all right.

00:49:23.100 --> 00:49:24.720
Well, and it's like, Locke will--

00:49:24.720 --> 00:49:29.510
It's like they'll commit
to like 40 minutes

00:49:29.510 --> 00:49:31.000
or 60 minutes with me.

00:49:31.000 --> 00:49:32.720
And I take it personally, it's like,

00:49:32.720 --> 00:49:34.060
that's all you can take of me.

00:49:34.060 --> 00:49:35.323
And once that's over, it's like

00:49:35.323 --> 00:49:36.156
"I'm gonna go back in my room."

00:49:36.156 --> 00:49:38.380
In order to watch
something else without me.

00:49:38.380 --> 00:49:40.520
But the thing that Locke will do

00:49:40.520 --> 00:49:44.540
is he and his friends
will, there'll be, he like,

00:49:44.540 --> 00:49:48.530
he texted me the other
night is with a friend.

00:49:48.530 --> 00:49:50.447
And he was like, "Dad, what
was the name of that movie

00:49:50.447 --> 00:49:52.360
"that we watched where
this and that happened?"

00:49:52.360 --> 00:49:54.479
I was like, send him the text.

00:49:54.479 --> 00:49:56.130
I was like, it was this.

00:49:56.130 --> 00:50:00.567
And then I pick him up
and he's like, I'm like,

00:50:00.567 --> 00:50:03.220
"Hey, did you watch a
movie, did you enjoy it?"

00:50:03.220 --> 00:50:05.220
He says, "Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah."

00:50:06.458 --> 00:50:07.548
I mean, I ask him about a part.

00:50:07.548 --> 00:50:08.880
He's like, "We didn't finish it."

00:50:08.880 --> 00:50:10.150
Didn't finish.

00:50:10.150 --> 00:50:11.231
"We didn't finish it."

00:50:11.231 --> 00:50:13.200
I'm like, "What you
didn't finish the movie."

00:50:13.200 --> 00:50:16.230
I don't understand how
the teenage brain works.

00:50:16.230 --> 00:50:18.820
And it wasn't because, it was just like,

00:50:18.820 --> 00:50:20.180
the night was over or whatever.

00:50:20.180 --> 00:50:23.310
And it's like, "We'll watch
the rest of it later."

00:50:23.310 --> 00:50:24.143
It's like I,

00:50:25.350 --> 00:50:28.210
again in theory, the
Quibi thing makes sense.

00:50:28.210 --> 00:50:31.320
It seems like it was the answer to that,

00:50:31.320 --> 00:50:32.660
but it just didn't,

00:50:32.660 --> 00:50:33.570
it hasn't worked yet.

00:50:33.570 --> 00:50:36.732
I feel like it still will
at some point, I don't know.

00:50:36.732 --> 00:50:41.732
Whoa, just, hey I gotta go down.

00:50:42.113 --> 00:50:43.970
We've been rewatching,

00:50:43.970 --> 00:50:46.970
and this is Lily's idea,
rewatching all the Marvel movies.

00:50:46.970 --> 00:50:49.330
She's a huge Marvel fan.

00:50:49.330 --> 00:50:53.000
And I was like, "This is
good, I like a game plan."

00:50:53.000 --> 00:50:55.130
The Niels are watching
all the Marvel movies.

00:50:55.130 --> 00:50:56.820
We went through all the Lord of the Rings

00:50:56.820 --> 00:50:59.630
and we're watching Survivor

00:50:59.630 --> 00:51:01.970
and we're watching the Lord of
the Rings behind the scenes.

00:51:01.970 --> 00:51:04.600
Like we have different things
that are ongoing and I,

00:51:04.600 --> 00:51:07.490
that is an appeal of series.

00:51:07.490 --> 00:51:10.130
And then she kind of applied
that to Marvel movies.

00:51:10.130 --> 00:51:11.890
So then it's like, we're
gonna watch all of these.

00:51:11.890 --> 00:51:13.350
She's so into them.

00:51:13.350 --> 00:51:14.850
But I'm like, girl, you gotta,

00:51:16.060 --> 00:51:17.910
graduate is not the right word.

00:51:17.910 --> 00:51:20.010
But it is what I kind of feel like.

00:51:20.010 --> 00:51:21.820
I'm a huge fan of all the Marvel movies.

00:51:21.820 --> 00:51:24.020
I love watching them,
but there's something,

00:51:26.021 --> 00:51:28.870
you gotta watch like good movies too.

00:51:28.870 --> 00:51:31.620
It's not what you would
call classic cinema.

00:51:31.620 --> 00:51:34.490
Yeah, cinematically to some more

00:51:35.450 --> 00:51:37.433
artistic approaches to things.

00:51:38.382 --> 00:51:41.270
Yeah, well, and I feel
like I broke that seal.

00:51:41.270 --> 00:51:43.883
I told you that Locke and
I watched Pulp Fiction.

00:51:45.200 --> 00:51:48.880
It was like, he kept leaning over,

00:51:48.880 --> 00:51:51.630
'cause I remember I watched
it when we were in college.

00:51:53.897 --> 00:51:58.130
And we remember, we talked
about this on the show

00:51:58.130 --> 00:51:59.540
when we talked about our favorite movies.

00:51:59.540 --> 00:52:01.230
And the way it like transformed

00:52:01.230 --> 00:52:02.820
what we thought was possible with a movie.

00:52:02.820 --> 00:52:03.920
So was he into it?

00:52:03.920 --> 00:52:05.990
He kept turning to me
and he was just like,

00:52:05.990 --> 00:52:08.290
God, see, like making those faces

00:52:08.290 --> 00:52:10.530
and saying these things
like this is so great.

00:52:10.530 --> 00:52:12.840
Like the very beginning
when they're having

00:52:12.840 --> 00:52:14.810
that conversation about
the Royale with cheese,

00:52:14.810 --> 00:52:16.360
like he just got this smile on his face.

00:52:16.360 --> 00:52:21.293
I was like, yes, he
understands why this is good.

00:52:22.690 --> 00:52:24.293
And since then he's,

00:52:25.490 --> 00:52:27.180
we've been watching a bunch of movies

00:52:27.180 --> 00:52:29.483
and from that time in my life.

00:52:31.394 --> 00:52:34.960
But still even when, Friday night,

00:52:34.960 --> 00:52:37.210
Saturday night rolls
around and he's at home.

00:52:38.690 --> 00:52:41.110
He don't want to be with you
on a couch for three hours.

00:52:41.110 --> 00:52:43.320
Yeah, even though I've introduced him

00:52:43.320 --> 00:52:45.610
to these great movies.

00:52:45.610 --> 00:52:47.360
It still is this thing that's like, it's,

00:52:47.360 --> 00:52:49.120
there's the threshold to overcome

00:52:49.120 --> 00:52:50.687
to get them to be like,

00:52:50.687 --> 00:52:55.400
"Yeah, I wanna sit down and
devote my evening to this."

00:52:55.400 --> 00:52:56.233
It's a lot.

00:52:56.233 --> 00:52:57.066
It's a lot that they would--

00:52:57.066 --> 00:52:58.720
I was like, let's watch a movie.

00:52:58.720 --> 00:53:00.570
You know what, tonight
we're gonna watch a movie.

00:53:00.570 --> 00:53:02.970
We're going to watch a movie.

00:53:02.970 --> 00:53:04.730
Lando is not here.

00:53:04.730 --> 00:53:07.610
Lando was at your house
for Shepherd's birthday.

00:53:07.610 --> 00:53:08.450
Just the two of them.

00:53:08.450 --> 00:53:09.400
And they watched a movie.

00:53:09.400 --> 00:53:10.680
And I was like, we can watch a movie

00:53:10.680 --> 00:53:12.550
that Lando can't watch.

00:53:12.550 --> 00:53:14.723
We're gonna watch The Reverent.

00:53:15.830 --> 00:53:17.460
Leonardo DiCaprio is--

00:53:17.460 --> 00:53:18.293
That's one on the list for me--

00:53:18.293 --> 00:53:22.897
Is amazing, this one, and
they were a little reticent,

00:53:25.860 --> 00:53:27.320
but I'm like, this is happening.

00:53:27.320 --> 00:53:31.500
It's a good movie, you're gonna like it.

00:53:31.500 --> 00:53:35.403
It's not too long into it
that there's the bear attack.

00:53:36.460 --> 00:53:40.990
And Lily like stands up.

00:53:40.990 --> 00:53:45.877
And I honestly, I had
forgotten how traumatic,

00:53:47.616 --> 00:53:51.710
I mean, how real this and relentless.

00:53:51.710 --> 00:53:53.240
I mean, I wouldn't call it a bear attack.

00:53:53.240 --> 00:53:55.340
I would call it three bear attacks.

00:53:55.340 --> 00:53:58.480
I don't know if you remember,
we saw it in the theater.

00:53:58.480 --> 00:54:00.430
And that was the only time I'd seen it.

00:54:01.695 --> 00:54:03.542
The bear attacks at the
dude three different times.

00:54:05.130 --> 00:54:08.177
And after the first time
you're like, "Oh my gosh,

00:54:08.177 --> 00:54:12.667
"I just don't feel like
I can keep watching this.

00:54:12.667 --> 00:54:13.837
"I don't know if I can take this.

00:54:13.837 --> 00:54:16.030
"This is so intense."

00:54:16.030 --> 00:54:18.838
And so Lily is like, "I can't watch this."

00:54:18.838 --> 00:54:20.300
And you know what?

00:54:20.300 --> 00:54:21.133
She was like--

00:54:21.133 --> 00:54:23.590
Like sit down and watch
this guy get maimed.

00:54:23.590 --> 00:54:26.342
Yeah, I was like, well, listen,

00:54:26.342 --> 00:54:30.480
but I was like, you loved Us,

00:54:30.480 --> 00:54:32.070
like you watch all these,

00:54:32.070 --> 00:54:34.640
you watch stuff that's just as intense.

00:54:34.640 --> 00:54:36.470
Like there was, I mean,

00:54:36.470 --> 00:54:38.500
that was real and scary,
but yeah, it was--

00:54:38.500 --> 00:54:39.350
And gory.

00:54:39.350 --> 00:54:42.100
And gory, but so was Us,

00:54:42.100 --> 00:54:44.820
Us was in very scary and very gory.

00:54:44.820 --> 00:54:46.110
Us is gory.

00:54:46.110 --> 00:54:49.370
She's like, turns out it
was the intensity of it,

00:54:49.370 --> 00:54:51.700
but it was also, I didn't,

00:54:51.700 --> 00:54:53.932
and she's inching our way to the door now.

00:54:53.932 --> 00:54:55.460
And she's like, I just
don't wanna watch two guys

00:54:55.460 --> 00:54:57.270
chase themselves around,

00:54:57.270 --> 00:54:59.513
chase around each other on the wilderness.

00:55:00.560 --> 00:55:02.350
Chase themselves around the wilderness.

00:55:02.350 --> 00:55:03.920
I can't even put the sentence together,

00:55:03.920 --> 00:55:05.000
but you know what I'm saying?

00:55:05.000 --> 00:55:06.817
Chase went on wilderness.

00:55:06.817 --> 00:55:11.270
I'm like, well, I'm losing this battle.

00:55:11.270 --> 00:55:14.030
But then Christy and
Lincoln watched it with me.

00:55:14.030 --> 00:55:16.960
And I think Lincoln was like

00:55:16.960 --> 00:55:18.500
"That was a good movie."

00:55:18.500 --> 00:55:19.540
He watched the whole thing.

00:55:19.540 --> 00:55:21.004
We watched the whole thing.

00:55:21.004 --> 00:55:22.170
It's a long movie.

00:55:22.170 --> 00:55:24.800
It's a good movie cinematically, I mean--

00:55:24.800 --> 00:55:25.860
We wanna watch that one together.

00:55:25.860 --> 00:55:26.870
Pretty mind blowing,

00:55:26.870 --> 00:55:28.900
but it's very intense.

00:55:28.900 --> 00:55:33.200
To get to get back to games for a second.

00:55:33.200 --> 00:55:34.980
We're gonna play Catan again this weekend.

00:55:34.980 --> 00:55:37.070
I'm getting back on that horse.

00:55:37.070 --> 00:55:38.880
Now you talked about

00:55:38.880 --> 00:55:40.980
how you didn't think you
would be into strategy games.

00:55:40.980 --> 00:55:43.650
Now for the longest time,
I've always just thought,

